{"author":{"id":"a66e0db0201c515c5090d5e52c036411dda9ba2f138110a926616a18a8ea0dd6"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1790976847,"nonce":"/SrsObKNZHUDRQOoYw1hNUcYE6U=","target":"2574808899fb26b84572c3bf3760daabfa9659bc610ec7cb9fc05c668939c175","message":"`host::raise` does not return: HashLink's `hl_throw` longjmps to the Haxe trap, and Ash's does the same on wasm (std/src/error.rs, `throw_impl`, `longjmp` under `target_family = \"wasm\"`). Rust frames between the native and the trap are skipped, destructors included. A `MutexGuard` alive at the raise is never dropped, so the mutex stays locked.\n\ncrates/xgpu-backend/src/template/web.rs raises with the state lock held in two places:\n\n- `pass_open`, through `encoding`, when a pass is begun on an encoder that already has one open (also holding the encoder entry's own mutex);\n- `configure`, when the canvas cannot take the configuration's format.\n\nA program that catches either error and goes on blocks forever at its next GPU call, at `state()`. Uncaught, the program ends anyway, so this shows only when a program catches.\n\nThe native backend raises while holding an encoder entry's mutex: `rendering` in template/backend/render.rs (\"no render pass is open on this encoder\") and `compute_begin` in template/backend.rs (\"a pass is already open\"), among others that lock `held` first. That encoder is then locked for good; its table's lock is not, since `find!` drops it.\n\nFix: decide what to raise while locked, raise after the guards drop.","files":null}]}